Environment: WSL2 distro "netrewind-lab" (Alpine Linux 3.24.1), kernel
6.6.87.2-microsoft-standard-WSL2. This is the project's own documented "fast"
Linux environment (docs/dev-environment.md), already present on this machine
with a history of prior release/build work - used as-is, nothing new created
except a path-safe junction C:\netrewind-src -> "C:\My project\NetRewind"
(also documented) so paths do not have to be quoted through three shells.

$ wsl -d netrewind-lab -u root -- sh /mnt/c/netrewind-src/lab/check-kernel.sh
kernel:  6.6.87.2-microsoft-standard-WSL2
btf:     present (5908 KiB) - CO-RE will work
clang:   Alpine clang version 22.1.3
tp:      OK   sock/inet_sock_set_state
tp:      OK   tcp/tcp_retransmit_skb
tp:      OK   tcp/tcp_probe
tool:    OK   nft
tool:    MISS iptables
tool:    OK   ss

Result: BTF present, all three tracepoints the eBPF flow collector needs are
present, nft is installed. iptables is absent but not required (the policy
collector shells out to nft). This kernel can run the real collectors,
including eBPF, not just the netlink-only stubs.

$ wsl -d netrewind-lab -u root -- sh -c 'cd /mnt/c/netrewind-src && make bpf-check'
internal/collect/flow/bpf/flow.bpf.o matches internal/collect/flow/bpf/flow.bpf.c, and the source compiles

Result: the committed eBPF object is still faithful to its source, and the
source still compiles with the clang available here.
